&Q000401

{A.1 = 1-8}
A.1.	@wDN{Enlil}, the eminent one, the sovereign lord, whose utterance is trustworthy; @wDN{Nunamnir}, the eternal shepherd of the Land, 
A.2.	who hails from the great mountain; the great counsellor, the first and foremost in heaven and on earth, 
A.3.	who is in control of all the divine powers; lord, who is imbued with great fearsomeness in accordance with his nobility, 
A.4.	a perfected heavenly star, who takes good care of the primeval and choice divine powers, 
A.5.	who alone is the lofty god; lord, life-giving light, 
A.6.	who leads the people all over the world along one track; 
A.7.	huge net spread over heaven and earth, rope stretched over all the lands! 
A.8.	Who ever instructed @wDN{Enlil}, who ever rivalled him? 

{A.9 = 9-14}
A.9.	He thought up something of great importance and he made public what his heart, a mighty river, 
A.10.	carried: the hidden secrets (?) of his holy thought. 
A.11.	The matter is a holy and pure one, it concerns the divine powers of the @wTN{E-kur}, 
A.12.	the fated good brick embedded (?) in the bottom of the @fsux{abzu}, it is something most important: 
A.13.	a trustworthy man will rebuild the @wTN{E-kur}, thereby acquiring a lasting name. 
A.14.	The son of this trustworthy man will long hold the sceptre, and their throne will never be overthrown. 

{A.15 = 15-20}
A.15.	To that end, @wDN{Ašimbabbar} appeared shining in the @wTN{E-kur}, pleaded to his father @wDN{Enlil} and made him bring a childbearing mother (?); 
A.16.	in the @wTN{E-duga}, @wDN{Nanna}, the princely son, asked for the thing to happen. 
A.17.	The @fsux{en} priestess gave birth to the trustworthy man from his semen placed in the womb. 
A.18.	@wDN{Enlil}, the powerful shepherd, caused a young man to emerge: 
A.19.	a royal child, 
A.20.	one who is perfectly fitted for the throne-dais, @wRN{Šulgi} the king. 

{A.21 = 21-27}
A.21.	@wDN{Enlil} gave him a good name: 'A lion's seed, who provides the @wTN{E-kur} generously, 
A.22.	the beloved one of @wDN{Ninlil}; the one granted authority in the @wTN{E-kur}; the king of @wSN{Urim}, 
A.23.	the one with shining heart, the shepherd, the protective genius of the Land'. 
A.24.	@wDN{Enlil} chose @wRN{Šulgi} in his pure heart and entrusted the Land to him. 
A.25.	As the shepherd of all the countries, @wDN{Enlil} leant the crook and the staff against his arm, 
A.26.	and placed the immutable sceptre of @wDN{Nanna} in his hand; 
A.27.	he made him raise his head high, sitting on an unshakeable royal seat. 

{A.28 = 28-30}
A.28.	The day was for prayers, the night was for supplications; the Land rested in peace. 
A.29.	The shepherd of prosperity, @wRN{Šulgi}, he with a lasting name, the king of jubilation, 
A.30.	the mighty one, the semen engendered by the faithful man, praised @wDN{Enlil}. 

{A.31 = 31}
A.31.	This is the @fsux{sa-gida}. 

{B.32 = 32-33}
B.32.	@wRN{Šulgi}, the mighty king, ……, who exults in his triumph, 
B.33.	the …… king vanquished the rebellious land with the @fsux{agakar} weapon of @wDN{Inana}. 

{B.34 = 34}
B.34.	It is the @fsux{ŋišgiŋal} of the @fsux{sa-gida}. 

{C.35 = 35-43}
C.35.	May @wDN{Enlil} the trustworthy, 
C.36.	whose words are lofty -- good fate determined by him takes precedence -- who makes sturdy flax and barley grow -- may he prolong the life of @wRN{Šulgi}, 
C.37.	the provider of the @wTN{E-kur} -- hence its flax is indeed fine flax, 
C.38.	its barley is indeed fine barley -- the property of @wDN{Nanna}, 
C.39.	the houseborn-slave of the @wTN{E-kur}, 
C.40.	him whom @wDN{Ninlil} named at his birth @wRN{Šulgi}, 
C.41.	the shepherd of the Land, 
C.42.	the man whom @wDN{Enlil} knows, 
C.43.	the steward of the temple. 

{C.44 = 44-48}
C.44.	So that its voluminous offering meals would bring joy to the @wTN{E-kur}, 
C.45.	@wDN{Enlil} called upon @wSN{Urim}, the good city founded by the princely one, 
C.46.	the inside of which is a holy treasure chest which, like the @fsux{abzu}, 
C.47.	no eye can see -- the city of good purification rites and pure hand-washing rites; 
C.48.	and what he says is trustworthy. 

{C.49 = 49-53}
C.49.	For his setting up the abundant sacrificial tin cups, 
C.50.	for his having served evening meals in the dining-hall of the @wTN{E-kur}, 
C.51.	@wDN{Enlil} determined a princely fate for the shepherd, 
C.52.	him who is worthy of the kingship and the lofty throne-dais, 
C.53.	@wRN{Šulgi}, who is adorned with the @fsux{ba} garment of the @fsux{en} priests. 

{C.54 = 54-59}
C.54.	The one beautiful like heaven, ……, 
C.55.	@wDN{Nanna}, the king, 
C.56.	the ……, gave him good …… in the @wTN{Ki-ur}, 
C.57.	the great place. At the command (?) of @wDN{Enlil}, 
C.58.	he ……, he roars for him (?) against the foreign land that no one dare oppose. 
C.59.	He stabilizes the countryside, and the people lie at his feet. 

{C.60 = 60-62}
C.60.	@wRN{Šulgi}, the shepherd is the honey man beloved by @wSN{Nibru}; 
C.61.	may the true shepherd, @wDN{Šulgi}, 
C.62.	refresh himself in the pleasant shade of @wDN{Enlil's} brickwork! 

{C.63 = 63}
C.63.	It is the @fsux{sa-ŋara}. 

{D.64 = 64}
D.64.	O @wRN{Šulgi}, @wDN{Enlil} has brought forth happy days for you in your reign! 

{D.65 = 65}
D.65.	It is the @fsux{ŋišgiŋal} of the @fsux{sa-ŋara}. 

{E.66 = 66-68}
E.66.	Heaven's king, earth's great mountain, Father @wDN{Enlil}, heaven's king, 
E.67.	earth's great mountain, thought up something great: 
E.68.	he chose @wDN{Šulgi} in his heart for a good reign! 

{E.69 = 69}
E.69.	It is its @fsux{uru}. 

{E.70 = 70}
E.70.	An @fsux{adab} of @wDN{Enlil}. @notep{lines 69 and 70 are written as one line in source} 
